>
  <p>
    If your electrician Google Ads account is “converting” but the calendar is full of fan installs
    and other low-ticket work, check whether you’re on <strong>Maximize Conversions</strong>.
    Google’s recommended bid strategy optimises for the easiest conversions — not the jobs that pay
    for trucks. Multi-service trades get hurt first.
  </p>

  <h2>What is Max Conversions actually maximising?</h2>
  <p>
    Volume of whatever you told Google counts as a conversion. If that’s “form fill” or “phone call”
    with no quality filter, the algorithm will hunt the cheapest path to that event. Low-friction,
    low-ticket jobs convert easier than panel upgrades and commercial quotes. The account looks
    healthy in the ads UI while your average ticket quietly dies.
  </p>
  <div class="receipt">
    <div class="receipt-label">THE FAILURE MODE</div>
    <p>
      Homeowners search the trade (“electrician”) more than the specific job. Pair broad trade
      intent with a bid strategy hungry for easy conversions and you get a shotgun of the wrong
      work — busy techs, thin margins.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Why electricians are exposed</h2>
  <p>
    Electrical companies sell a wide mix: service calls, installs, upgrades, specialty work. One
    conversion action rarely means one profitable job type. Agencies that “turn on Smart Bidding”
    and leave for the month are outsourcing your job mix to an algorithm that does not care about
    your overhead.
  </p>
  <p>
    Same class of mistake as selling leads instead of booked jobs — the metric on the dashboard
    isn’t the metric in your bank account. We cover that pattern in
    <a href="/blog/bad-vs-good-agency">bad vs good agencies</a> and
    <a href="/blog/good-google-marketing">what good Google marketing looks like</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What to do instead</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Fix the foundation before fancy bidding.</strong> Wrong GBP category and a weak site
      will poison any strategy —
      <a href="/blog/google-category-relevance">Motha’s category fix</a> still applies.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Prove with Local Services Ads first</strong> when it fits — Hooked Up ran years of
      volume on an <a href="/blog/electrician-local-services-ads">LSA-led engine</a> before treating
      Search as the hero.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Define conversions as outcomes you’ll pay for.</strong> Qualified booked calls beat
      raw form dumps. Our LSA QA model (~$25 per booked call we review) exists for that reason.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Use Smart Bidding only when the account has clean signal.</strong> Junk in, junk
      optimised. Early on, tighter structure beats a recommended default.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <blockquote>
    A converting account full of the wrong jobs is not a successful account.
  </blockquote>

  <h2>How this shows up on a sales call</h2>
  <p>
    Owners say “ads don’t work for electricians.” Often ads work fine — at finding cheap
    conversions. The fix isn’t always more budget. It’s better definitions, better landing paths,
    and a partner who will turn a “recommended” strategy off when it’s quietly wrecking job mix.
  </p>
